itative universal Pyrosequencing methylation analysis of CpG sites</article-title><source><italic>BioTechniques</italic></source><year>2003</year><volume>35</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>146</fpage><lpage>150</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12866414</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mikeska</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bock</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>EI-Maarri</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Optimization of quantitative MGMT promoter methylation analysis using Pyrosequencing and combined bisulfite restriction analysis</article-title><source><italic>Journal of Molecular Diagnostics</italic></source><year>2007</year><volume>9</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>368</fpage><lpage>381</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17591937</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cankovic</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Mikkelsen</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Rosenblum</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><name><surname>Zarbo</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A simplified laboratory validated assay for MGMT promoter hypermethylation analysis of glioma specimens from formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue</article-title><source><italic>Laboratory Investigation</italic></source><year>2007</year><volume>87</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>392</fpage><lpage>397</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17260000</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><label>18</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Derks</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Lentjes</surname><given-names>MH</given-names></name><name><surname>Hellebrekers</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name><name><surname>de Bru&#x000ef;ne</surname><given-names>AP</given-names></name><name><surname>Herman</surname><given-names>JG</given-names></name><name><surname>van Engeland</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Methylation-specific PCR unraveled</article-title><source><italic>Cellular Oncology</italic></source><year>2004</year><volume>26</volume><issue>5-6</issue><fpage>291</fpage><lpage>299</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15623939</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><label>19</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Esteller</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hamilton</surname><given-names>SR</given-names></name><name><surname>Burger</surname><given-names>PC</given-names></name><name><surname>Baylin</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name><name><surname>Herman</surname><given-names>JG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Inactivation of the DNA repair gene <italic>O</italic><sup>6</sup>-<italic>methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase</italic> by promoter hypermethylation is a common event in primary human neoplasia</article-title><source><italic>Cancer Research </italic></source><year>1999</year><volume>59</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>793</fpage><lpage>797</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10029064</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><label>20</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Harden</surname><given-names>SV</given-names></name><name><surname>Sanderson</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Goodman</surname><given-names>SN</given-names></name><name><surname>Partin</surname><given-names>AAW</given-names></name><name><surname>Walsh</surname><given-names>PC</given-names></name><name><surname>Epstein</surname><given-names>JI</given-names></name><name><surname>Sidransky</surname><g